items.description:
Effective and efficient office administration lies at the heart of every successful organisation. Working in an office requires one to have a multitude of skills to work effectively. This training programme is intended to enable learners to understand the key areas of administration and carry out routine office processes, office and online ethics, office automation, effective communication for the efficient management of an office and hence an organisation. It will also address business documentation and payment procedures, information and record handling procedures and preparations for meetings and business travel. It also enhances competencies that the learner might already have leading to the learner to be more professional in the implementation of his/her duties regarding administration work and personal assistance to managerial roles.
The learner will be able to: 
a)	Describe the structure and functions within an organisation;
b)	Identify the different roles of staff within the principal departments of an organisation;
c)	Understand basic employment law, key employment terms and their significance and employer and employee rights and obligations;
d)	Identify relevant documents needed for registration or termination of employment/dismissal of employment;
e)	Describe health and safety procedures relevant to working in an office environment;
f)	Understand the systems, procedures and methods in use in an office for storing and classifying information;
g)	Describe procedures for dealing with incoming or outgoing mail/parcels/goods;
h)	Understand how to use appropriate office equipment, office applications and online platforms, and routine procedures and standards;
i)	Understand the basic fundamentals of book-keeping in the context of a variety of settings;
j)	Describe procedures for the purchase and sale of goods including credit control, reconciling invoices and making and recording payments for them; The learner will be able to: 
a)	Demonstrate knowledge of the structure and functions within an organisation;
b)	Demonstrate knowledge of the different roles of staff within the principal departments of an organisation;
c)	Practice basic employment law, key employment terms and their significance and employer and employee rights and obligations;
d)	Use relevant documents needed for registration or termination of employment/dismissal of employment;
e)	Apply principles of good practice for health and safety procedures relevant to working in an office environment;
f)	Apply the systems, procedures and methods used in an office for storing and classifying information;
g)	Apply procedures for dealing with incoming or outgoing mail/parcels/goods;
h)	Apply principles of good practice when operating office equipment, office applications and online platforms, and routine procedures and standards;
i)	Practice the fundamentals of book-keeping procedures met on a day to day basis in the accounting environment of any business; 
j)	Apply principles of good practice  when purchasing and selling goods including credit control, reconciling invoices and making and recording payments for them;
k)	Apply principles of good practice when dealing with business documentation and payment procedures;
l)	Apply principles of good practice in simple stock control procedures;
m)	Apply principles of good practice when using information and record handling handling systems;
